      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by expressing the integer in terms of its remainder when divided by 15. Let's denote the integer by \\( n \\). According to the problem, we have:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. We will do this by evaluating \\( n \\) modulo 3 and modulo 5 separately.\n\nFirst, consider the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 3, we only need to consider the remainder when 7 is divided by 3. We have:\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\nNext, consider the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 5, we only need to consider the remainder when 7 is divided by 5. We have:\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. We have:\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThe sum of these remainders is:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
