      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Since an integer \\( n \\) when divided by 15 gives a remainder of 7, we can express \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5.\n\n**Step 1: Find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3.**\n\nSince \\( n = 15k + 7 \\), we observe that:\n\\[ 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\]\nThus,\n\\[ 15k + 7 \\equiv 0 + 7 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\nTo find the remainder of 7 when divided by 3, we perform the division:\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\n**Step 2: Find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5.**\n\nSimilarly, since \\( n = 15k + 7 \\), we observe that:\n\\[ 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThus,\n\\[ 15k + 7 \\equiv 0 + 7 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\nTo find the remainder of 7 when divided by 5, we perform the division:\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\n**Step 3: Find the sum of the remainders.**\n\nNow that we have the remainders:\n- The remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n- The remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nThe sum of these remainders is:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nHence, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
